OmniLanManFS filename encoding: the "<xxxx" escape
===================================================

On SMB2 mounts OmniLanManFS represents Unicode characters outside Latin-1 in RISC OS filenames with the "<xxxx" escape.
This page is the reference for RISC OS software that interprets or produces such names: a Unicode-aware Filer, a text
editor, a Wimp icon renderer.

Context
-------

SMB2/3 servers store filenames as UTF-16LE. RISC OS filenames are Latin-1 (alphabet 101), with a small number of
characters carrying special meaning to FileSwitch ('.', ':', '$', '&', '%', '@', '^', '<', '>', '#', space). UTF-16
covers the whole of Unicode; Latin-1 covers 256 characters, of which RISC OS reserves around a dozen.

When LanManFS reads a directory listing from an SMB2 server, each UTF-16 code unit from U+0000 to U+00FF is presented to
RISC OS as the byte of the same value, except the C1 control characters U+0080 to U+009F. Each character that
alphabet 101 places at bytes 0x80 to 0x9F (the Euro sign, the curly quotes and so on) is presented as that byte. Every
other code unit (the C1 controls, CJK, emoji, Greek, Cyrillic, most of Latin Extended, and so on) must still be
representable in some form, or the file could not be reached from the Filer. The "<xxxx" escape is that representation.

The encoding
------------

Grammar:

    escape ::= '<' hex hex hex hex
    hex    ::= '0'..'9' | 'a'..'f' | 'A'..'F'

Each escape represents exactly one UTF-16 code unit (not one Unicode code point). A Unicode character above U+FFFF,
which on the wire is a UTF-16 surrogate pair, is therefore written as two consecutive escapes:

    U+1F600 (grinning face emoji)  ->  "<d83d<de00"

The encoder always emits lowercase hex digits, always exactly four of them, and never follows the escape with a closing
'>'. The decoder accepts upper, lower or mixed case, but producers should use lowercase consistently.

There is deliberately no closing bracket. In FileSwitch, a sequence "<name>" triggers system-variable expansion; "<name"
does not. The absence of '>' is what makes the escape safe to embed in a filename that passes through FileSwitch's path
resolver.

Round trips, and where they fail
--------------------------------

On the way from the server, '&', '@', '^', '%', '<' and '>' are also presented as escapes: "<0026", "<0040",
"<005e", "<0025", "<003c" and "<003e". A RISC OS path gives each of them a meaning of its own: '%' would otherwise be
shown as '>', and a '>' after a '<' earlier in the path would close it into a "<...>" that FileSwitch expands. A '$' is
presented as '<', as on SMB1, unless four hex digits follow it; then it is presented as "<0024", so "$RECYCLE.BIN" is
shown as "<RECYCLE/BIN" and "05_$1000_Wedding.flac" as "05_<00241000_Wedding/flac". Every other character up to U+00FF
is presented as itself, apart from the C1 controls above.

On the way to the server, a name LanManFS finds in the directory is sent in the server's own spelling. In a name it does
not find (one being created, for instance) a '<' followed by four hex digits is kept and decoded as an escape, and any
other '<' is sent as '$'.

So a '<' in a name from the server followed by four hex digits always starts an escape, any other '<' stands for '$', a
name from the server never contains a '>', and a name that came from the encoder reaches the server as the object it
came from.

The SMB1 mapping
----------------

*LMNameMode -SMB2LegacyChars presents '$', '&', '@', '^' and '%' from an SMB2 server as '<', '+', '=', ',' and '>', as on
an SMB1 mount, and *LMNameMode -SMB2EscapedChars returns to escapes. Under the SMB1 mapping a '<' is ambiguous: a server
name containing '$' followed by four hex digits, such as "$beef", is presented as "<beef". An existing file of that name
is still reached, because LanManFS finds the name in the directory and sends the server's own spelling, but a program
decoding escapes reads it as U+BEEF, and a new name typed as "<beef" is created as the single character U+BEEF, not as
"$beef". A '%' later in the same path is presented as '>', which closes the '<' into a "<...>" that FileSwitch expands,
so such a path cannot be reached at all. A '&', '@' or '^' is presented as '+', '=' or ',', which goes back to the
server as itself, so such a file is listed but cannot be opened by the name shown.

Usage by Unicode-aware applications
-----------------------------------

Applications that present, edit or generate filenames with code points outside Latin-1 can use this encoding as
the contract for names passed to and from LanManFS:

- Decoding for display. When reading a name from a LanMan path, scan for '<' followed by four hex digits and substitute
  the corresponding Unicode character. Combine two consecutive escapes in the surrogate range (U+D800..U+DFFF) into one
  supplementary-plane code point.

- Encoding for use. When constructing a name to hand back to LanManFS (rename, create, and so on), convert any
  character outside the RISC OS Latin-1 repertoire into the corresponding "<xxxx" form. Write supplementary-plane code
  points as two escapes, one per UTF-16 surrogate half.

Deliberate edits to the hex digits are legal. To rename a file from one emoji to another, write the new escape sequence
directly. LanManFS does not check "<xxxx" sequences at the rename boundary: the encoding is a data format that
applications may edit.

Scope and limits
----------------

The escape exists because FileSwitch paths are Latin-1 byte strings.

On an SMB1 mount nothing decodes or produces the escape: names are exchanged as single-byte strings, '$', '&', '@' and
'^' are presented as '<', '+', '=' and ',', and every '<' in a new name is turned into '$'.

The escape sits alongside the separate c/Xlate translation step that handles the other characters RISC OS reserves ('#',
'%', space, and so on). The two meet only at '<': on an SMB2 mount c/Xlate passes a '<' that starts an escape through
unchanged, and maps any other '<' to '$'.

Reference implementation
------------------------

c/SMB2Unicode in the OmniLanManFS source tree. The two directions are:

- smb2_utf16le_to_latin1 - wire UTF-16LE to RISC OS Latin-1 plus escapes. Produces "<xxxx" for every surrogate half, for
  the C1 controls U+0080 to U+009F, for any other code unit above U+00FF that alphabet 101 does not place at bytes 0x80
  to 0x9F, and, in names from a listing or a change notification, for '&', '@', '^', '%', '<', '>' and a '$' that
  four hex digits follow.
- smb2_latin1_to_utf16le - RISC OS Latin-1 plus escapes to wire UTF-16LE. Recognises "<xxxx" and decodes it as a raw
  UTF-16 code unit.

The test c/Xlate applies before keeping a '<' (xlt_hex_digits, in Xlt_CopyViaInverseTable) is the same one the decoder
applies, and the same one the encoder applies to the characters after a '$' (dollar_reads_as_escape).

The Latin-1 to Unicode table is loaded at module initialisation from Service_International 8 (alphabet 101) on RISC OS
versions that support it, falling back to a compiled-in copy of RISC OS 5's alphabet 101 otherwise. The reverse table,
used on every name from the server, is always the compiled-in RISC OS 5 layout.
